Dash runtime and development tools
==================================

This ebuild is based on sci-visualization/dash in ::gentoo. It builds the
Python package offline from the upstream PyPI source distribution, including
the upstream built JavaScript assets and Jupyter extensions. No npm command
runs during an ebuild phase.

USE=development installs dash-generate-components, dash-update-components,
renderer, and a complete, version-matched upstream source archive. The flag
changes installed files, rather than just selecting optional dependencies.

renderer initializes a writable checkout under
${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/dash/4.5.0/dash-4.5.0 on first use. It never
rebuilds or overwrites the installed system package. Alternatively, select
your own checkout with renderer --path=/path/to/dash/dash-renderer.
renderer --help lists the available commands. renderer build downloads npm
dependencies and extra React assets, then builds the JavaScript bundles in
that workspace. Network access is needed for this user-invoked development
operation; it is not part of the Portage build.

For dash-update-components, change into a writable full Dash checkout first
(the renderer workspace above is suitable). Initialize the root workspace
according to upstream CONTRIBUTING.md, then build the component libraries:

  npm ci
  dash-update-components all --ci True --concurrency 1

USE=development provides Black, which the generated Python backend build
scripts invoke. The combined root, renderer and component lockfiles require
Node 22.22.1 or later within 22.x, 24.x, or 26.x, with npm enabled. In
particular, Lerna does not accept intervening odd-numbered Node releases.
The command copies generated libraries into this writable checkout; use
that checkout or an isolated editable environment to run the modified Dash.

dash-generate-components operates on your own component project; its npm
dependencies likewise belong to that project's writable development tree.

Optional runtime integrations remain installed and available without USE
flags. Install/world-select the corresponding packages when needed:

  HTTP compression: dev-python/flask-compress
  Async Flask callbacks: dev-python/asgiref
  Disk cache: dev-python/diskcache, dev-python/multiprocess,
              dev-python/psutil, dev-python/msgspec
  Redis storage: dev-python/redis (>=3.5.3, <=8.1.0), dev-python/msgspec
  Celery callbacks: dev-python/celery (>=5.1.2, <5.7),
                    dev-python/kombu (<5.7), dev-python/redis as above
  FastAPI backend: dev-python/fastapi, dev-python/uvicorn
  Quart backend: dev-python/quart
  Shared-storage serialization: dev-python/msgspec

Upstream requests uvicorn[standard]. Consult Uvicorn's own documentation for
the optional providers needed for its server features.

The former compress and diskcache USE flags from ::gentoo are unnecessary:
they only selected optional runtime dependencies and changed no Dash files.
This follows Gentoo QA policy PG0001. Existing installed providers continue
to work; world-select them if you want to keep them through dependency cleanup.

Plotly Cloud and Dash AG Grid are separate upstream projects. The plotly
command delegates to plotly-cloud and requires that separate package and,
where applicable, a service account. Enterprise services are not included
in the open-source Dash distribution.
